\A{pgpkeys} Verifying KiTTY++ downloads

\I{verifying new versions}KiTTY++ releases carry no \i{GPG signatures}.
Three other checks protect them. Every program file and every installer
carries an \i{Authenticode} signature. Every portable package ships a
\c{SHA256SUMS} file. The programs check themselves when they start.

Download KiTTY++ only from the release page:
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}{\c{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}}

(None of this has anything to do with SSH keys. These checks only
concern the origin of the files.)

\H{pgpkeys-signed} What is signed

Every \c{.exe} file of a release is Authenticode-signed: in the
installers, in the 64-bit and 32-bit portable ZIPs and on the ISO image.
Both \c{.msi} installers are signed as well. The signer to expect is:

\c KAPPER NETWORK-COMMUNICATIONS GmbH

The signatures carry a trusted timestamp.

The ZIP files and the ISO image are containers without a signature of
their own. Check the files inside them after unpacking. The other files
in a package (the help file, \c{kitty.ini.example}, \c{README-BETA.md})
are not signed. The \c{SHA256SUMS} file covers them.

\H{pgpkeys-authenticode} Checking the signature

In Windows Explorer, right-click the file, choose \q{Properties} and
open the \q{Digital Signatures} tab. The list must show KAPPER
NETWORK-COMMUNICATIONS GmbH. Select that entry and click \q{Details}.
The dialog must report \q{This digital signature is OK.} A file without
a \q{Digital Signatures} tab carries no signature.

In PowerShell:

\c Get-AuthenticodeSignature kitty.exe |
\c   Format-List Status, SignerCertificate

\c{Status} must be \c{Valid}. The certificate subject must begin with
\c{CN=KAPPER NETWORK-COMMUNICATIONS GmbH}. The same command checks an
installer: give it the \c{.msi} file instead.

The built-in update check runs a downloaded installer only after the
same test: a valid signature chain from the expected publisher. A file
that fails is deleted without being run.

\H{pgpkeys-sha256sums} Checking the SHA256SUMS file

Each portable ZIP and each folder of the ISO image contains a
\c{SHA256SUMS} file. It lists the SHA-256 hash of every file in the
package, one line per file. The 32-bit package also contains
\c{SHA1SUMS} for tools that know SHA-1 only.

To check one file, open a command prompt in the unpacked folder:

\c certutil -hashfile kitty.exe SHA256

or, in PowerShell:

\c Get-FileHash kitty.exe -Algorithm SHA256

Compare the result with the line for that file in \c{SHA256SUMS}.
Upper or lower case does not matter. \c{README-BETA.md} in each package
contains a one-line command that checks every listed file at once. It
prints \c{OK} or \c{FAIL} per file. Every line must read \c{OK}.

Windows XP has no built-in SHA-256 tool. Check the 32-bit package on a
newer Windows first, then copy the checked folder to the XP system.

The installers are not listed in a \c{SHA256SUMS} file. Their
Authenticode signature is the check for them.

\H{pgpkeys-selfcheck} The programs check themselves

A release build checks its own file at every start.

\b The file name must begin with the program's own name or the matching
PuTTY name, for example \c{kitty} or \c{putty} for \c{kitty.exe}. Case
does not matter. \c{kitty (1).exe} and \c{kitty-0.85.exe} start
normally. Under any other name the program stops with a message like
\q{\cw{Renamed executable: this program runs only as kitty*.exe.
Found: 6bovxvce.exe}}.

\b The program checks its own Authenticode signature. It refuses to run
only when the file was modified after signing, when the signature was
removed or when somebody else signed it again. The message reads like
\q{\cw{Signature check failed: this program is not signed by its
publisher. Found: bad digest}}.

\lcont{

It never refuses because a machine cannot judge the signature. An old
or unpatched Windows may not know the signing algorithm, may lack the
root certificate or may have no revocation data. Windows XP and an
offline Windows 7 are examples. KiTTY++ starts normally there.
Nothing is fetched from the network for this check.

}

\b Every shipped program carries a release stamp: the SHA-256 hash of the
file as released, signed with an Ed25519 key made for that release
only. This check needs no Windows cryptography. It also works where
Authenticode cannot judge. A file that no longer matches refuses to
start with a message like \q{\cw{Integrity check failed: this program
file was changed after its release. Found: modified}}.

\lcont{

Other findings are \c{bad stamp} (the stamp signature is not from the
release key), \c{no stamp} and \c{truncated}. The message also goes to
the Windows Application event log under the source \c{KiTTY++}. When
the program cannot read its own file, a visible program starts anyway.
A program nobody can see refuses with \c{cannot check}, in the event
log only.

}

\c{kitty_pterm.exe} and \c{kitty_tel.exe} carry the stamp but do not
check themselves. Builds compiled from source carry no signature, no
stamp and no check.

\q{Application > Security > Applications} in the configuration box
lists the KiTTY++ program files beside the running program. It shows
for each one whether its stamp and its signature check out. A red row
is a file that was changed, signed by somebody else, lacks a check or
belongs to another version.

These checks make tampering harder. They are not a security boundary:
somebody who removes the check code gets a working file.

\H{pgpkeys-fail} When a check fails

Do not run the file. Delete the download and fetch it again from
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}{\c{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}}.

If a file from that page fails a check, report it privately as a
security issue. Use GitHub private vulnerability reporting at
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/security}{\c{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/security}}
(\q{Report a vulnerability}). Do not open a public issue for it.
Include the file name, the release version and the exact message or
check result.

\H{pgpkeys-pgpfp} The \c{-pgpfp} option

The \i\c{\-pgpfp} command-line option is inherited from PuTTY. It
prints the \i{PGP key fingerprint}s of the PuTTY master keys for
historic reasons only. They prove nothing about a KiTTY++ file. They
are only useful for checking downloads from the PuTTY project itself.
The option is deprecated and will be removed in a future release.
